[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: The hook layer as middleware: every joint is the pre or post of one operation

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-HOOK-MIDDLEWARE-INTERFACE
TITLE: The hook layer as middleware: every joint is the pre or post of one operation
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
PROPOSED, NOT ACCEPTED. A snapshot of the hook layer as it stood on
2026-08-29, so it can be picked apart later rather than re-derived.

A JOINT is an event a handler can be attached to. Every joint is the PRE or
the POST of one OPERATION. A pre handler may STOP the operation; a post
handler is handed the result and cannot. Stop is expressed the way a CLI or a
Python program already expresses it -- a non-zero exit or a raised exception
-- with one exit code (or one exception class) reserved for "refused" so a
refusal and a handler bug read differently. The reason travels as the message.

THE OPERATIONS.

  scribe.command   arguments received, nothing loaded yet
  scribe.load      every .sdoc read into memory
  scribe.change    one edit applied in memory; kind = create, delete, set,
                   unset, relate, unrelate, move
  scribe.write     new bytes computed, about to hit disk / on disk
  scribe.reload    the canon re-read from disk to prove it parses
  scribe.read      show, list, check
  git.commit       pre = pre-commit, message = commit-msg, post = post-commit
  git.push         pre = pre-push
  git.rewrite      post only (amend, rebase)
  git.merge        post only
  git.checkout     post only
  strictdoc.index  post only: strictdoc's own hook, fired after every index
                   build, including inside every scribe load

devenv is not a joint. It is where hooks are declared and installed.

THE OPTION SURFACE.

  ai.strictdoc.hooks.<name> = {
    on      = "scribe.change";     # an operation from the list above
    phase   = "pre";               # pre | post
    run     = <package>;           # a command: the event as JSON on stdin
    # python = "pkg.mod:function"; # or in-process, for scribe and strictdoc
    timeout = 30;
  };
  ai.strictdoc.hooks.gitTarget = "prek";   # prek | files | a function

THE HANDLER PROTOCOL, the same for a command and a Python function. In: one
JSON object carrying operation, phase, the actor if an extractor is
configured, and the operation's payload (for scribe.change: node uid, kind,
field, old value, new value; for git.push: the ref range). Out: exit 0 or a
return means watch; exit 1 or raise Refuse("why") means stop; anything else
is a handler bug and also stops, reported as a bug.

WHAT THE MODULE DOES WITH IT. Scribe calls its hooks directly. Git hooks are
rendered by the chosen target into prek configuration or raw hook files; the
target also supplies what git does not -- the project root (hooks are shared
across worktrees) and a scrubbed GIT_DIR. The strictdoc hook is registered in
strictdoc_config.py.

THREE ENABLERS THAT DO NOT EXIST YET. An actor slot, where one configured
extractor (an ssh key, an environment variable) runs once per command and its
answer is handed to every event; scribe writes a literal today. The old value
on a field change, dropped today. A typed refusal, a bare string today.

Scribe run from a shell and scribe imported as a library fire the same joints
(DEC-SCRIBE-NAMES-THE-WRITER); today only the shell path has all of them.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
